cleared when the scope/app ends. See
[RustCrypto implementation](https://docs.rs/chacha20poly1305/0.11.0/chacha20poly1305/).

Imports are explicit dry-run selections from a versioned source snapshot. Read
only selected work/nonsecret metadata; never follow credential-file references.
Reencrypt selected work into a new v2 generation, validate it, then atomically
commit the v2 manifest with a restartable import receipt. Preserve the previous
v2 generation for rollback. Never mutate source bytes or copy credentials,
sessions or approvals. Require fresh sign-in/account verification before importing
account-bound learner work; caches/downloads are regenerated. An interrupted import
cannot expose a partially committed generation; unsupported downgrade fails
before touching data. C23 validates every failure/commit point.

## 4. Managed workbench execution

Keep RequestDocument semantics and immediate React editor truth separate from
execution permission. Send captures fresh complete input; native admission resolves
variables and references, validates method/URL/headers/body and constructs one
immutable effective request before attaching managed secrets.

The credential class is explicit: tenant-managed scan/management/Gateway grants,
workbench-owned secrets, or anonymous. Never infer the class from redactions.
Replace v1's trust in the editable `prisma-airs-scm` collection ID: no imported
ID, request file, tenant reference or metadata flag grants access to tenant
secrets. Native authority registers explicit tenant/workspace capabilities for
the selected operation. Changing a collection ID cannot create that registration.
Classify every requested tenant reference as managed before resolving it,
independent of collection provenance. In particular, admit a managed OAuth token
destination before obtaining client secrets; edited token_url variables cannot
send an existing grant to a new origin. Legitimate endpoint changes follow the
explicit profile/capability destination-review flow, invalidating old authority.
Known AIRS operations use a versioned method/path/destination catalogue:

- Known reads: admit only with their actual capability and approved destination.
- Known mutations: require a Configuration review of the exact effective request.
- Unclassified managed requests, including unknown reads: deny without dispatch;
  do not offer a renderer 'override' or blindly review an unknown operation.
- Classification also checks registered Prisma origin/method/path independent of
  credential class. Manually entered workbench credentials cannot bypass review
  for known configuration mutations; unknown operations at registered managed
  origins are denied until catalogued. Redaction is not classification.
- Generic HTTP to other destinations with workbench-owned credentials/anonymous
  access retains operator-directed behavior and cannot borrow tenant credentials.
  Arbitrary third-party proxy semantics cannot be inferred universally; this rule
  covers native-managed destinations and explicitly registered Prisma origins.

A workbench review includes expanded URL/query, method, material headers, body/
file hashes, cookie snapshot, resolved variable revision, credential revision,
destination and resource/action classification. Secret values are never included
in the review/model/log projection. Dispatch rechecks all bindings; changed
input/files/environment/credentials/cookies/context requires a new review.
Reject ambiguous path normalization, encoded separators/traversal, duplicate auth
headers, URL user-info and attacker-controlled managed proxy/Host overrides.

Disable automatic redirects for managed requests in the first implementation.
Show the 3xx result; following it is a new explicitly prepared/admitted request,
including same-origin redirects. Workbench-owned redirects retain bounded generic
HTTP behavior with explicit credential/cookie origin rules. This is stricter than
v1's redactions-based cross-origin redirect guard and must be disclosed/tested.

Remove v1's automatic post-401 resend for all workbench requests. Token refresh
may prepare a new explicit Send; it never repeats the customer operation. Lost
responses/timeouts remain uncertain after dispatch. No auto-replay on reconnect,
credential refresh, history replay, imported requests or engine suggestion.
The 193 catalogue entries (81 mutation-method specifications) are metadata parity,
not a claim of 193 dedicated workflows or live qualification.

## Validation and remaining gates

desktop-host owns the OS process-spawn/process-group adapter and browser opener;
desktop-core Harness owns lifecycle and conversation policy through those seams.
C19 sidecar_supervision host tests cover actual spawn/cleanup, and C04/C20/C22
browser_callback host tests cover approved OS-browser opening/callback bounds.
- **T07 Lifecycle:** bounded concurrent work, cancellation, queue backpressure,
  superseding reads, app close/restart, sleep/wake, expired credentials, and
  monotonic versus wall-clock decisions. UI remains responsive during work.

## Presentation and Academy

- **T08 Shared presentation:** web and desktop consumers build from the same
  reviewed shared source; themes/fonts/routes render correctly. General desktop
  screens mount no Academy gamification/account effects or web manager routes.
- **T09 Native UI:** actual Tauri command path, menus/shortcuts, keyboard-only
  workflow, focus restoration, VoiceOver labels/order, zoom/large text, themes,
  reduced motion, long/empty/error content, and window resizing. Mock IPC tests
  alone cannot establish these. macOS WebDriver availability must be verified;
  use supported native accessibility/automation or recorded manual steps.
- **T10 Academy identity:** device grant, pending/slow_down/expiry/cancel, refresh,
  revocation, wrong issuer/client/scope, account switch/sign-out, and stale replies.
  Token management stays Rust-owned. The learner account is independent of tenant.
- **T11 Learning authority:** client bundles contain no answer keys/private lab
  evaluation, privileged routes, or assessor tokens. Forged verdict/award/local
  exam clock cannot pass; the hosted backend decides access, grading, and awards.
  Signed-in denial differs from guest sign-in; sandbox never uses tenant secrets.
- **T12 Offline work:** download lease/revision/account access, encrypted draft
  save/restart, locked vault, sign-out/account switch, withdrawn content, duplicate
  submission IDs, changed draft versions, and reconnection. No deferred action
  replay. Valid local work remains usable without claiming offline grading.

## Workflows and Harness

- **T13 Collection semantics:** exact capability subsets, large collections,
  bounded pagination/truncation, search/filter/page resets, stale/partial cache,
  consistent Home/Inbox source facts, and no false complete/zero/absent claims.
- **T14 API Client documents:** edit/save/conflict while typing, invalid input,
  environment/secret separation, import/path traversal/size errors, multipart,
  cookies/redirect auth, cancellation, history/download, Git/profile adoption,
  and recovery from interrupted writes. Arbitrary workbench targets cannot borrow
  privileged application credentials through renderer-supplied references.
- **T15 Harness process:** actual native binary and bounded bootstrap/JSONL
  protocol, malformed/oversized frames, correlation/duplicates, queue pressure,
  crash/EOF/cancel, context change, graceful/forced cleanup, and missing/tampered
  resources. No PATH launcher fallback or plaintext rollout/history.
- **T16 Harness authority:** typed investigation/proposal tools, active-draft
# C15 — API Client execution, history, downloads, and SCM catalogue

Phase: P04. Status: planned. Required predecessor: C14.

## Outcome

Complete the generic HTTP workbench with explicit native authority.

## Inputs and dependency gates

C14; Rust HTTP execution and complete reviewed request catalogue metadata.

Read the [common execution contract](../IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md),
[review protocol](../review-protocol.md), and [test catalogue](../test-catalogue.md).
Concrete interfaces follow accepted G01 ADRs. Read the [selected design](../../architecture/DESIGN.md),
[qualification contract](../../architecture/qualification.md) and this chunk's
exact target in [checks.json](../../architecture/checks.json). Freeze all source/shared dependency
inputs; active neighboring worktrees and unavailable methods cannot count as pins.

## Milestones

1. Implement request execution, body/auth/variables, multipart, cookies, response/history/download and cancellation.
2. Known Prisma configuration mutations and unclassified requests using managed AIRS credentials cannot bypass Rust prepare/review/Apply; define conservative raw-request admission in G01.
3. Enforce exact credential destination/redirect rules, file bounds and scoped secrets; arbitrary workbench targets cannot borrow app capabilities.
4. Preserve all agreed working built-in collection requests as specifications; advertise live qualification only for actually tested operations.

## Native admission details

Follow ADR0004. Imported SCM collection IDs/tenant references cannot create a
native credential capability. Admit token_url and request destinations before
resolving tenant client secrets. Disable managed automatic redirects and all
post-401 automatic workbench resend; new Send requires fresh admission. Include
edited/imported identity, token destination, same-origin redirect, stale files/
cookies/variables and lost-response negative tests.

## Required acceptance

- Production React Send tests cover fresh full input. Manual credentials cannot bypass registered-Prisma mutation review; token destinations are admitted before secret resolution; native proxy/TLS policy and no-401-resend are tested.

- Any changed UI also requires T09 actual native keyboard/focus/accessibility checks; global requirements are not deferred solely to C24.

- T02–T07,T14,T19: raw-request mutation bypass attempt, stale request draft, auth redirect, blocked destination, large/streamed body and cancel.
- Independent HTTP fixture server validates actual requests/cookies/multipart; no automatic replay or secret persistence in history.
- Native send/review/result/download/history flows with Node/Bun absent; supported catalogue count/options checked against pinned source.

G01/C01 must bind these assertions to exact commands and fixture/native targets
before execution. Run appropriate regressions when changes reach earlier chunks.
No test result, native acceptance, or live qualification is claimed by this file.

## Evidence and completion

T23 is mandatory here for this feature's local work and reconnect behavior: offline launch/read/edit, explicit retry, preserved uncertain outcomes, and no replay of pending reviews/scans/inference/HTTP mutations/tool calls. C24 repeats the cumulative matrix.

Before implementing/measuring this chunk's new workload, commit its numeric budget, dataset, hardware/OS and method under T20 governance. Record rationale and owner or independent reviewer acknowledgement for any change; C24/C26 use the pre-registered hash and retain original failures.

Exact raw-request admission policy, fixture traces, catalogue inventory and native complete workbench evidence.

Use the [implementer prompt](../prompts/implement-chunk.md) and
[independent reviewer prompt](../prompts/review-chunk.md). Require ≥9/10 overall
and in architecture, behavior, authority/security, and validation; no required
fixes or absent mandatory evidence. Preserve attempts, raw verdicts, hashes, and
remediation. Commit only the reviewed tree; do not unlock the next chunk early.

## Safety and recovery

Loopback fixture only in test configuration. No arbitrary customer endpoint traffic or collection-wide destructive execution.

